{% comment %}
  blocks/_expert-quote-image.liquid

  One of the photographs scattered either side of the expert in
  `sections/expert-quote.liquid`.

  ## Why it is a block and not two image pickers on the section

  The drawing has exactly two, so two image pickers would have been the shorter
  route. It would also have been a section that can never show three products,
  can never show one, and needs a code change either way. A block costs the
  merchant one click and buys them the whole range.

  ## Placement is `order`, not source position

  `start` and `end` become an `order` in `assets/base.css` — 1 before the centre
  column, 3 after it. Nothing here moves in the markup, which matters because
  the source order is what a screen reader announces and what the Tab key
  follows. A merchant dragging a picture from one side to the other should not
  be rewriting the order the page is read in.

  `start` and `end` rather than "left" and "right" for the same reason every
  other layout setting in the theme is spelled that way: they follow the text
  direction, so an Arabic storefront gets the arrangement mirrored rather than
  the arrangement broken.

  ## The drop is margin, not translate

  A `translate` would have been tidier to write and would have let the picture
  hang out of the bottom of the section, because a translated box still takes up
  its untranslated space and the row would size itself to where the picture is
  not. `margin-block-start` is in flow, so a picture dropped 120px makes the row
  120px taller and the section grows to hold it.

  Negative values are allowed, and are how a picture is lifted up beside the
  expert rather than sitting below it — the arrangement the drawing has.

  ## The loop is a class, not a bundle of numbers

  Four styles, shared with `blocks/_expert-quote-feature.liquid` and defined
  once in `assets/base.css`:

    Float   rises and falls. The quietest, and the one that reads as floating.
    Sway    tips around whatever tilt the picture already has.
    Zoom    breathes in and out.
    Orbit   traces a small circle. The most visible of the four.

  The distance and the speed come from the section, so every picture in it moves
  to one rhythm and a merchant tunes the whole composition from one place. The
  style and the offset into the cycle are per-picture, because pictures rising
  and falling in unison is a lift rather than a drift.

  Each style animates a different property — `translate`, `rotate`, `scale` —
  and never `transform`, which is what lets the reveal keep using `transform`
  and lets Sway rotate around the picture's own resting tilt instead of
  replacing it.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign b = block.settings

  assign placement = b.placement | default: 'start'
  if placement == 'end'
    assign order = 3
  else
    assign order = 1
  endif

  assign width = b.width | default: 260 | plus: 0
  assign width_mobile = b.width_mobile | default: width | plus: 0
  assign radius = b.radius | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign radius_mobile = b.radius_mobile | default: radius | plus: 0

  comment
    `plus: 0` after `default`, and `default` not used to supply a fallback for
    the mobile drop: 0 is a value a merchant chooses here — it is the picture
    sitting level with the top of the row — and `default` would replace it with
    the desktop stagger, which is the one arrangement a phone cannot use.

    So the desktop drop falls back to 0 and the mobile one falls back to the
    desktop value only when the setting does not exist at all, which is a block
    saved before the mobile slider existed.
  endcomment
  assign offset = b.offset | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign offset_mobile = b.offset_mobile | default: offset | plus: 0

  assign rotate = b.rotate | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the outline ----

    The same off-register echo the featured picture carries, offered here too so
    a merchant can use it on any of the pictures rather than only the one the
    drawing happened to put it on. Drawn only when it has both a style and a
    width — an outline 0px wide with a colour on it is three declarations that
    paint nothing.
  endcomment
  assign outline_style = b.outline_style | default: 'none'
  assign outline_width = b.outline_width | default: 0 | plus: 0

  assign has_outline = false
  if outline_style != 'none' and outline_width > 0
    assign has_outline = true
  endif

  if has_outline
    assign outline_percent = b.outline_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if b.outline_color == blank
      assign outline_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: outline_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign outline_alpha = outline_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign outline_paint = b.outline_color | color_modify: 'alpha', outline_alpha
    endif
  endif

  assign motion = b.motion_style | default: 'none'

  comment
    The picture is never wider than its own slider, so the `sizes` hint is that
    number rather than a viewport fraction. The browser is told the one thing
    that is actually true about the box, and stops fetching a 1600px variant for
    a 260px frame.
  endcomment
  assign sizes = width_mobile | append: 'px'
  assign sizes = '(min-width: 750px) ' | append: width | append: 'px, ' | append: sizes

  assign classes = 'expert-quote-pic expert-quote-pic--scatter expert-quote-pic--' | append: placement
  if motion != 'none'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' expert-quote-pic--' | append: motion
  endif
  if has_outline
    assign classes = classes | append: ' expert-quote-pic--outlined'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture block_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: b, declarations: true -%}
  --eqp-order: {{ order }};
  --eqp-width: {{ width }}px;
  --eqp-width-mobile: {{ width_mobile }}px;
  --eqp-offset: {{ offset }}px;
  --eqp-offset-mobile: {{ offset_mobile }}px;
  --eqp-rotate: {{ rotate }}deg;
  --eqp-radius: {{ radius }}px;
  --eqp-radius-mobile: {{ radius_mobile }}px;
  {%- comment -%}
    The picture's offset into the loop, written negative so it starts already
    that far in rather than standing still for that long first. The section owns
    the distance and the speed; this is the only part of the cycle each picture
    decides for itself.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --eqp-motion-delay: -{{ b.motion_delay | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}ms;
  {%- if has_outline -%}
    --eqp-outline-width: {{ outline_width }}px;
    --eqp-outline-style: {{ outline_style }};
    --eqp-outline-color: {{ outline_paint }};
    --eqp-outline-x: {{ b.outline_offset_x | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
    --eqp-outline-y: {{ b.outline_offset_y | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<motion-effect
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ block_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'image', block: b, target: '.expert-quote-pic__frame' %}
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div class="expert-quote-pic__frame">
    {%- comment -%}
      `placeholder` so the block is a picture-shaped box the moment it is added
      rather than an empty gap the merchant has to imagine the layout around —
      the same reason `sections/featured-offer.liquid` asks its background for
      one.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'image',
       image: b.image,
       ratio: b.ratio,
       sizes: sizes,
       placeholder: 'product-apparel-1',
       wrapper_class: 'expert-quote-pic__media' %}
  </div>
</motion-effect>

